id=3D"content"><article class=3D"style_learnContent__K5K7M"><header><h1>Sum=
 Between Range</h1></header><div class=3D"style_problemDescription__T4cPe">=
<p>Given an integer array, write a function which returns the <strong>sum o=
f values between two indexes</strong>.</p><h4 id=3D"example">Example:</h4><=
figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center;=
"><img alt=3D"Image represents three examples of a `sum_range` function ope=
rating on a numerical array `[3, -7, 6, 0, -2, 5]`.  Each example shows the=
 array with indices 0-5 displayed below each element.  The function `sum_ra=
nge(start_index, end_index)` calculates the sum of elements within a specif=
ied range (inclusive). The first example, `sum_range(0, 3)`, highlights ele=
ments at indices 0, 1, 2, and 3 (3, -7, 6, 0) in a light-blue box, with a b=
lue arrow above indicating their sum, `sum =3D 2`. The second example, `sum=
_range(2, 4)`, highlights elements at indices 2, 3, and 4 (6, 0, -2), showi=
ng `sum =3D 4` above the highlighted section. Finally, `sum_range(2, 2)` hi=
ghlights only the element at index 2 (6), resulting in `sum =3D 6`.  In all=
 cases, the function's parameters (start and end indices) are clearly visib=
le, along with the resulting sum displayed above the highlighted portion of=
 the array." width=3D"380" height=3D"175" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1=
" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com/images/courses/coding-patterns/prefix-sums/=
sum-between-range/sum-between-range-YTKLP363.svg" style=3D"color: transpare=
nt;"></div></figure><pre><code class=3D"hljs language-python">Input: nums =
=3D [<span class=3D"hljs-number">3</span>, -<span class=3D"hljs-number">7</=
span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">6</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">0<=
/span>, -<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
5</span>],
       [sum_range(<span class=3D"hljs-number">0</span>, <span class=3D"hljs=
-number">3</span>), sum_range(<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span c=
lass=3D"hljs-number">4</span>), sum_range(<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</sp=
an>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>)]
Output: [<span class=3D"hljs-number">2</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">=
4</span>, <span class=3D"hljs-number">6</span>]
</code></pre><h4 id=3D"constraints">Constraints:</h4><ul>
<li>
<p><code>nums</code> contains at least one element.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Each <code>sum_range</code> operation will query a valid range of the in=
put array.</p>
</li>
</ul></div>
<h2 id=3D"intuition">Intuition</h2>
<p>We need to code a function <code>sum_range(i, j)</code>, where <code>i</=
code> and <code>j</code> are the indexes defining the boundaries of the ran=
ge to be summed up.</p>
<p>A naive solution is to iteratively sum the array values from index <code=
>i</code> to <code>j</code>, which takes linear time for each call to <code=
>sum_range</code>. Since we have access to the input array before any calls=
 to <code>sum_range</code> are made, we should consider if any <strong>prep=
rocessing</strong> can be done to improve the efficiency of <code>sum_range=
</code>.</p>
<p>This problem deals with subarray sums, so it might be useful to think ab=
out how <strong>prefix sums</strong> can be applied to solve it. Consider t=
he integer array below and its prefix sums:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a code snippet illustrating the concept of =
prefix sum.  The top line declares a Python list named `nums` containing th=
e integer values [3, -7, 6, 0, -2, 5].  Below this,  faint gray numbers [0,=
 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] are shown as indices for the `nums` list. The second line d=
eclares another list named `prefix_sum`. This list contains the prefix sums=
 of the `nums` list.  `prefix_sum`'s values are calculated cumulatively: th=
e first element is the same as the first element in `nums` (3), the second =
is the sum of the first two elements of `nums` (3 + (-7) =3D -4), the third=
 is the sum of the first three (3 + (-7) + 6 =3D 2), and so on.  Similarly,=
 faint gray numbers [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5] are shown as indices for the `prefix=
_sum` list, aligning with the corresponding elements in `nums`.  The arrang=
ement visually demonstrates the relationship between an original list and i=
ts prefix sum, showing how each element in `prefix_sum` is the cumulative s=
um of the preceding elements in `nums`." loading=3D"lazy" width=3D"337" hei=
ght=3D"77" decoding=3D"async" data-nimg=3D"1" src=3D"https://bytebytego.com=
/images/courses/coding-patterns/prefix-sums/sum-between-range/image-10-01-1=
-VMKJURRC.svg" style=3D"color: transparent;"></div></figure>
<p>We already notice that the prefix sum array has some use: the prefix sum=
 up to any index <code>j</code> essentially gives the answer to <code>sum_r=
ange(0, j)</code>. For example, the sum of the range [0, 3] is just the pre=
fix sum up to index 3:</p>
<figure class=3D"py-1"><div style=3D"display: flex; justify-content: center=
;"><img alt=3D"Image represents a visual explanation of prefix sum calculat=
ion.  The top line shows the equation `sum_range(0, 3) =3D prefix_sum[3]`, =
indicating that the sum of elements from index 0 to 3 in an array is equal =
to the value at index 3 in the prefix sum array. Below, an array named `num=
s` is shown with values [3, -7, 6, 0, -2, 5], with indices 0 through 5 disp=
layed beneath each element. A light-blue background highlights elements at =
indices 0, 1, 2, and 3. A cyan-colored line segment above the highlighted p=
ortion indicates the range of summation, labeled 'sum =3D 2,' representing =
the sum of the highlighted elements (3 + (-7) + 6 + 0 =3D 2). A downward ar=
row points from the highlighted section of `nums` to an array named `prefix=
_sum`.  `prefix_sum` contains the cumulative sums of `nums`; its values are=
 [3, -4, 2, 2, 0, 5], with indices 0 through 5 displayed beneath each eleme=
nt. The value at index 3 in `prefix_sum` (2) matches the calculated sum of =
`nums` from indices 0 to 3, visually demonstrating the prefix sum concept."=
